Q: Pros and cons of native queries in JPA/Spring Data?

Native query = real SQL of the database (`nativeQuery = true`), not JPQL.

@Query(
  value = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE status = :status",
  nativeQuery = true
)
List<User> findActive(@Param("status") String status);

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Pros

1) Full SQL power
   Window functions, CTEs, DB JSON operators, hints, vendor functions.

2) Performance / tuning
   Use EXPLAIN-friendly SQL, indexes, DB-specific plans when JPQL is awkward.

3) Legacy SQL / stored procedures
   Reuse existing scripts, reporting SQL, procs.

4) Vendor features
   Full-text, specific pagination tricks, dialect functions JPQL doesn’t expose cleanly.

────────────────────────────────────────
❌ Cons

1) Vendor lock-in
   MySQL SQL may not run on Postgres/Oracle without changes.

2) Weaker portability & maintainability
   Table/column renames in entities don’t update native SQL automatically.

3) Less ORM safety net
   Mapping is easier to break (column aliases must match).
   You can still map to entities/DTOs, but it’s more manual/fragile
   (`@SqlResultSetMapping`, interface/DTO projections, careful aliases).

4) Mostly runtime failure
   Bad native SQL often fails when executed (or on first use),
   while JPQL is more often validated earlier at startup.

5) SQL injection risk IF you concatenate strings
   Native and JPQL are both safe with `:params` / `?`.
   Danger is `"WHERE name=" + input` — never do that.

6) Easy to bypass good repository design
   Huge SQL in Java reduces readability; prefer Flyway views / named queries for complex reporting.

Nuance (don’t oversell):
- Native queries are not “no caching always.”
  If they return managed entities, normal persistence context rules still apply.
- Lazy-loading issues are about entity graphs, not “native disables lazy” magically.
- Spring Data `Pageable` often removes need for handwritten LIMIT/OFFSET.

────────────────────────────────────────
When to use what

| Need                              | Prefer        |
| --------------------------------- | ------------- |
| Normal CRUD / entity queries      | JPQL / derived methods / Criteria |
| Complex report SQL, CTE, windows  | Native        |
| DB-specific optimization          | Native        |
| Keep app DB-portable              | JPQL/Criteria |

────────────────────────────────────────
⚖️ Conclusion / interview answer

“Use JPQL/Criteria for most entity work. Use native SQL when you need DB-specific features, heavy reporting SQL, or proven optimized queries.
Tradeoff is portability and maintainability — always bind parameters, map results carefully, and keep native SQL limited to cases JPQL can’t handle cleanly.”
